window.PORTAL={"day": 10, "title": "Hypoglycemia + Electrolytes + Metabolic Emergencies", "docTitle": "", "subtitle": ["NEET-SS + ICP Paediatrics — Day 10", "Hypoglycemia, Electrolytes & Metabolic Emergencies in the Newborn"], "source": "source/Day_10_NEET_SS_ICP_Paediatrics_FINAL_Expert_Merged_V5.docx", "pages": [{"id": "overview", "title": "Overview", "group": "Start", "kind": "overview", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>NEET-SS + ICP Paediatrics — Day 10</strong>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Hypoglycemia, Electrolytes &amp; Metabolic Emergencies in the Newborn"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>FINAL EXPERT-MERGED V5 • 8 October 2026 • 75 base MCQs + 2 extra notebook retests • 8 extra calculation drills</strong>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Conceptual anchors: Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics 22nd ed.; for neonatal depth cross-read Avery’s Diseases of the Newborn, Cloherty &amp; Stark’s Manual of Neonatal Care and NNF/IAP protocols. Guideline overlay: CPS 2025, PES 2015, international hyperinsulinism guideline 2024, NICE NG29, RCH. Doses are exam anchors; bedside prescribing follows local NICU protocol."}, {"t": "callout", "label": "", "title": "", "html": ["<strong>HOW TO USE DAY 10<br>Read mechanisms and algorithms → complete visual stations → do Active Recall → attempt Q1–Q77 → check Section C → complete the separate Calculation Drill.<br>Q19 and Q54 are notebook retests and are not part of the base 75. Use R1/R2/R3 and RED/AMBER/GREEN for repair.</strong>"], "fill": "E8F1F6"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Nelson 22nd provides the conceptual framework. CPS 2025, PES, international HI guidance, NICE and neonatal electrolyte guidance are kept as named frameworks. Numeric values are exam anchors; exact bedside drug preparation, electrolyte replacement and fluid composition must follow the current unit formulary."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Retest</strong>", "<strong>Recovered concept</strong>", "<strong>Stage</strong>", "<strong>If correct</strong>", "<strong>If incorrect</strong>", "<strong>Score</strong>"], "rows": [["Q19", "Day 7 Q34 — raw EEG evolution confirms seizure; aEEG trend alone is insufficient", "R1", "Advance to R2 at next scheduled window", "Mark x2; repair and re-test before R2", "Extra"], ["Q54", "Day 8 Q41 — preterm enteral energy/protein target interpretation", "R1", "Advance to R2 at next scheduled window", "Mark x2; reopen nutrition anchors and repeat", "Extra"]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "RAG rule: GREEN = correct and can explain the discriminator; AMBER = correct with hesitation/partial reasoning; RED = incorrect, guessed, or unable to explain why the closest distractor is wrong."}]}, {"title": "Learning Outcomes", "h1": "Learning Outcomes", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Separate transitional from persistent hypoglycemia using the named guideline, age and symptoms.", "Calculate GIR and dextrose concentration, including two simultaneous infusions and fluid restriction.", "Use the critical sample to separate hyperinsulinism, FAOD, hypopituitarism/adrenal disease, GSD I and galactosemia; know the HI treatment ladder.", "Manage hypocalcemia, hypo-/hypermagnesemia, hyper-/hypokalemia and phosphate disorders safely.", "Interpret sodium by tonicity, volume and urine osmolality; apply correction-rate limits and hypertonic-saline rescue.", "Classify acid-base disorders (anion gap, Winter’s formula) and run a hyperammonemia emergency including dialysis triggers."]}]}]}, {"id": "part-1", "title": "Mechanisms, Diagnosis & Management", "eyebrow": "Part I", "group": "Study Notes", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "1. First 60 seconds: jittery, apnoeic or seizing neonate", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Stabilise airway, breathing, circulation and temperature. Check a bedside glucose immediately; send a laboratory sample only if it does not delay rescue. In parallel consider calcium (ionised), magnesium, sodium, potassium, infection, HIE and intracranial pathology. <strong>Treat symptomatic hypoglycemia without waiting for the laboratory result.</strong> Jitteriness is stimulus-sensitive and stops when the limb is held; seizures do not, and subtle events need electrographic confirmation (raw EEG with aEEG)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Mechanism chain: impaired substrate supply / excess insulin / counter-regulatory failure → low plasma glucose → brain fuel deficit → irritability, apnoea, lethargy, seizure → immediate glucose → confirm recovery → investigate recurrence.</em>"}]}, {"title": "2. Hypoglycemia: compare frameworks before memorising a number", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Framework</strong>", "<strong>Population</strong>", "<strong>Exam anchor</strong>", "<strong>Meaning</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>CPS 2025</strong>", "At-risk, birth–72 h", "&lt;2.6 mmol/L (47 mg/dL)", "Intervention threshold in the transitional period; feed ± gel or IV by severity."], ["<strong>CPS 2025</strong>", "≥72 h", "Target ≥3.3 mmol/L (60 mg/dL)", "Persistent phase; critical sample when POC &lt;2.8 mmol/L."], ["<strong>PES 2015</strong>", "High-risk &lt;48 h", "Target &gt;50 mg/dL", "Treatment goal, not a universal IV-bolus trigger."], ["<strong>PES 2015</strong>", "High-risk &gt;48 h", "Target &gt;60 mg/dL", "Evaluate suspected persistent disorders."], ["<strong>PES 2015</strong>", "Suspected congenital disorder", "Target &gt;70 mg/dL", "Higher target while alternative fuels are impaired."], ["<strong>AAP 2011</strong>", "0–4 / 4–24 h", "Older operational thresholds", "Historical; do not mix with CPS/PES."], ["<strong>India: NNF/IAP</strong>", "Local protocols", "Confirm local pathway", "Do not claim thresholds identical to international documents."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>At-risk infants:</strong> IDM, SGA/IUGR, LGA, preterm/late preterm, perinatal stress, maternal beta-blocker or labetalol exposure, poor feeding, syndromic infants (Beckwith–Wiedemann, Sotos). Screening is not routine for the healthy term AGA infant. Meters are inaccurate at low values; laboratory confirmation is needed for persistent disease, but a symptomatic infant is never made to wait."}]}, {"title": "3. Glucose therapy: route, dose, GIR and drug anchors", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["<strong>Well, asymptomatic at-risk (CPS):</strong> feed + 40% dextrose gel 0.5 mL/kg (200 mg/kg) buccally; recheck in about 30 minutes.", "<strong>Symptomatic, severe, or enteral failure:</strong> IV access, D10 2 mL/kg (200 mg/kg) over a few minutes, then continuous infusion (start about 6–8 mg/kg/min); recheck in 30 minutes.", "<strong>GIR (mg/kg/min)</strong> = D% × mL/kg/day ÷ 144, or D% × 10 × mL/h ÷ (kg × 60). Needed D% = GIR × 144 ÷ mL/kg/day.", "When fluid-restricted, raise glucose by <strong>concentration</strong>, not water. Above about D12.5 use a central line.", "GIR &gt;8 mg/kg/min is a clue to hyperinsulinism (suggests, does not prove)."]}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Agent</strong>", "<strong>Typical anchor dose</strong>", "<strong>Comment</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Glucagon</strong>", "IM 0.03 mg/kg (max 1 mg) bolus; infusion 10–20 µg/kg/h", "Needs liver glycogen; poor in SGA/preterm. Used as a bridge in HI."], ["<strong>Hydrocortisone</strong>", "Stress-replacement dosing per neonatal formulary after cortisol sample", "Use for suspected/proven adrenal insufficiency or hypopituitarism; not routine therapy for hyperinsulinism."], ["<strong>Diazoxide</strong>", "5–15 mg/kg/day PO in 2–3 doses", "First-line for established HI; assess fluid retention and pulmonary-hypertension risk. A thiazide is commonly co-prescribed."], ["<strong>Octreotide</strong>", "Specialist protocol; commonly 5–25 µg/kg/day SC/IV divided", "Second line for diazoxide-unresponsive HI; monitor for tachyphylaxis and NEC risk."], ["<strong>Surgery / PET</strong>", "¹⁸F-DOPA PET-CT then focal resection or near-total pancreatectomy", "For diazoxide-unresponsive HI, usually KATP (ABCC8/KCNJ11); focal form can be cured."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Verify all doses against your current formulary; ranges differ between units."}]}, {"title": "4. Persistent hypoglycemia: critical sample and pattern recognition", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Obtain at glucose &lt;50 mg/dL (before corrective treatment if it will not delay care): glucose, BOHB, FFA, lactate, bicarbonate/gas, ammonia, insulin and C-peptide, cortisol, GH, acylcarnitine/amino acids (serum and urine). An undetectable insulin does not exclude HI: the <strong>fuel pattern and glucagon response</strong> show insulin action."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Condition</strong>", "<strong>GIR / glucose</strong>", "<strong>BOHB</strong>", "<strong>FFA</strong>", "<strong>Lactate / gas</strong>", "<strong>Other discriminators</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Hyperinsulinism</strong>", "GIR &gt;8; glucagon rise ≥30 mg/dL", "&lt;1.8 (low)", "&lt;1.7 (low)", "Normal", "Insulin detectable (≥2 µU/mL) or not; macrosomia; KATP/GCK/GLUD1/HADH."], ["<strong>GLUD1 HI-hyperammonemia</strong>", "As HI", "Low", "Low", "Normal", "Ammonia persistently 100–200 µmol/L; diazoxide-responsive; no scavengers."], ["<strong>FAOD (e.g. MCAD)</strong>", "Fasting-triggered", "Low (hypoketotic)", "High", "May be normal", "High CK/transaminases, cardiomyopathy; raised C8/C10; sibling SIDS."], ["<strong>Hypopituitarism / adrenal</strong>", "Recurrent", "Often normal–high", "High", "Normal", "Midline defect, micropenis, cholestasis; low cortisol/GH."], ["<strong>GSD type I</strong>", "Fasting", "Raised", "High", "Lactate ↑↑, uric acid ↑, TG ↑", "Hepatomegaly; glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency."], ["<strong>Galactosemia</strong>", "After milk feeds", "Variable", "Variable", "Variable", "Jaundice, E. coli sepsis, cataract; urine reducing substance positive; stop lactose."], ["<strong>Organic acidemia</strong>", "Variable", "High (ketosis)", "High", "High anion gap", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, ± hyperammonemia."]]}]}, {"title": "5. Calcium, magnesium, phosphate", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Early hypocalcemia (&lt;72 h): prematurity, IDM, asphyxia. Late (&gt;72 h): phosphate load, vitamin D deficiency, hypomagnesemia, hypoparathyroidism (DiGeorge with conotruncal heart disease, thymic hypoplasia and low PTH; maternal hyperparathyroidism). Ionised calcium is active; total calcium falls with low albumin. Symptomatic hypocalcemia (seizures, apnoea, stridor, prolonged QTc): 10% calcium gluconate 1–2 mL/kg (100–200 mg/kg salt) slowly over 10–30 minutes on a cardiac monitor with a secure line; avoid mixing with bicarbonate; extravasation causes necrosis; calcium chloride is for central access/resuscitation only."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Pattern</strong>", "<strong>Mechanism / clues</strong>", "<strong>Next step</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Early low Ca</strong>", "Preterm / IDM / birth stress; immature PTH", "Check ionised Ca; assess symptoms, phosphate, Mg."], ["<strong>Late low Ca + high P</strong>", "Phosphate load; hypoparathyroid physiology", "Review feed; Mg, PTH, vitamin D; low-phosphate feed."], ["<strong>Low total, normal ionised Ca</strong>", "Hypoalbuminemia", "Do not give IV calcium on total calcium alone."], ["<strong>Refractory low Ca + low Mg</strong>", "PTH secretion and action impaired", "Replace Mg (IV magnesium sulfate, monitored) with calcium."], ["<strong>Low Ca + cardiac defect + thymic hypoplasia</strong>", "22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge)", "FISH/microarray; PTH; echo."], ["<strong>High Mg (maternal MgSO₄)</strong>", "Hypotonia, apnoea, ileus, hypotension", "Support ventilation; IV calcium gluconate if compromised."], ["<strong>Preterm low P + high ALP</strong>", "Mineral deficit / metabolic bone disease", "Increase phosphate and calcium; monitor ALP."], ["<strong>Low P + K + Mg on early aggressive PN (IUGR)</strong>", "Refeeding-type intracellular shift", "Add phosphate (and K/Mg) to PN."]]}]}, {"title": "6. Sodium and water", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Hyponatremia:</strong> confirm hypotonicity (exclude pseudohyponatremia and correct for hyperglycemia: add 1.6 mmol/L per 100 mg/dL glucose above 100), assess volume and urine osmolality/sodium. SIADH: euvolaemic, low plasma osmolality, concentrated urine, urine Na high. Cerebral salt wasting: hypovolaemic with high urine Na. Salt-wasting CAH: hyponatremia + hyperkalemia + hypoglycemia, ambiguous or hyperpigmented genitalia, high 17-OHP; give saline, dextrose and stress-dose hydrocortisone after drawing cortisol/17-OHP. <strong>Seizures or severe symptoms:</strong> 3% saline about 2–3 mL/kg over 10–20 minutes to raise sodium 4–6 mmol/L, then slower correction (about ≤8–10 mmol/L in 24 h)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Hypernatremia:</strong> chronic cases adapt with brain osmolytes, so correct no faster than about 0.5 mmol/L/h and 10–12 mmol/L/day, with sodium every 4–6 h. If shocked, restore perfusion with isotonic fluid first. Concentrated urine = intact ADH axis (insufficient intake, extrarenal loss); dilute urine = diabetes insipidus; weight gain with oedema = sodium overload (flushes, drug diluents, bicarbonate). Rapid fall in hypernatremia risks cerebral oedema; rapid rise in chronic hyponatremia risks osmotic demyelination."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image1.png", "alt": "Sodium trajectories\nPlan A 0.4 mmol/L/h versus plan B 1.2 mmol/L/h"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Data plot (educational, not a patient record): the dashed line is too fast and risks cerebral oedema."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>NICE NG29</strong> concerns term neonates and older children; it does not cover preterm infants. In the first days many neonates need little or no sodium until postnatal diuresis; individualise by gestation, weight trend, urine output, serum sodium and glucose."}]}, {"title": "7. Potassium (new)", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Topic</strong>", "<strong>Key points</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Hyperkalemia thresholds</strong>", "Confirmed non-haemolysed K⁺ &gt;6.5 mmol/L is a common neonatal treatment threshold; ECG change at any level is an emergency. Repeat a suspected haemolysed heel-prick result from free-flowing blood."], ["<strong>Non-oliguric hyperkalemia of prematurity</strong>", "&lt;30 weeks, day 1–3, good urine output; transcellular shift (immature Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase), ECF contraction, acidosis."], ["<strong>Other causes</strong>", "Haemolysis, renal failure, CAH, IVH/bruising, exchange/blood transfusion, tissue breakdown, excess intake."], ["<strong>ECG sequence</strong>", "Peaked T → P flattening → wide QRS → sine wave → VF/asystole."], ["<strong>Treatment order</strong>", "1) <strong>10% calcium gluconate</strong> about 1 mL/kg over 5–10 min on ECG monitor (membrane stabilisation). 2) <strong>Shift:</strong> salbutamol nebulised/IV, or insulin 0.1 U/kg with D10 2 mL/kg over 30 min (watch for hypoglycemia). 3) Bicarbonate only if acidotic. 4) <strong>Remove:</strong> furosemide if urine output present, dialysis if refractory. Rectal resin is avoided in preterms (NEC)."], ["<strong>Hypokalemia</strong>", "Flat T, U waves, ileus; furosemide, amphotericin, vomiting, Bartter/Gitelman; check Mg. Replace at a controlled IV rate on a monitor; never push."]]}]}, {"title": "8. Acid–base, hyperammonemia and inborn errors", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "AG = Na − (Cl + HCO₃); normal about 8–16 (albumin-dependent). High AG: lactate, ketones, organic acids; normal AG: bicarbonate loss with chloride gain. <strong>Winter’s:</strong> expected PaCO₂ = 1.5 × HCO₃ + 8 ± 2; measured above range = additional respiratory acidosis; below = additional respiratory alkalosis. Respiratory alkalosis plus encephalopathy: send ammonia urgently (free-flowing, on ice, analyse at once)."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Feature</strong>", "<strong>Urea-cycle disorder</strong>", "<strong>Organic acidemia</strong>", "<strong>Other</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>Gas</strong>", "Respiratory alkalosis", "High-AG metabolic acidosis, ketosis", "FAOD: no ketosis; PDH: lactic acidosis"], ["<strong>Ammonia</strong>", "Very high (&gt;500)", "Moderate (often &lt;300)", "THAN: preterm, early, very high, transient; GLUD1: mildly raised"], ["<strong>Blood count</strong>", "Normal", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia", "—"], ["<strong>Citrulline / orotic acid</strong>", "OTC: citrulline low, orotic acid high. CPS1/NAGS: citrulline low, orotic acid low. ASS: citrulline very high. ASL: argininosuccinate.", "—", "MSUD: maple syrup odour, leucine, ketoacidosis"]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Metabolic crisis sequence:</strong> ABCs and glucose → ammonia, gas, lactate, ketones, amino acids, urine organic acids, acylcarnitine (do not delay treatment) → stop protein only 24–48 h → anti-catabolic calories (10% dextrose ± lipid; insulin if hyperglycemic) → <strong>scavengers:</strong> sodium benzoate and sodium phenylacetate loading 250 mg/kg each in D10 over 90–120 min, then maintenance; arginine 200 mg/kg (OTC/CPS1) or 600 mg/kg (ASS/ASL); carglumic acid for NAGS deficiency or organic acidemia → <strong>dialysis</strong> (haemodialysis preferred; CVVHDF if unavailable) when ammonia is about &gt;400 µmol/L (or &gt;300 with coma), or rising despite scavengers. Peritoneal dialysis is the least effective."}]}]}, {"id": "tables", "title": "Tables & Algorithms", "group": "Quick Reference", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "Algorithm 1 — Low glucose in the newborn", "h1": "Part II — Clinical Decision Algorithms", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Stabilise; bedside glucose now.", "Symptomatic or severe → IV D10 2 mL/kg then infusion; confirm laboratory glucose without delay.", "Well at-risk → CPS pathway: feed ± 40% gel; recheck at about 30 minutes; IV if feed refused or failure.", "Recurrent / persistent / GIR &gt;8 → critical sample, HI–endocrine–metabolic work-up.", "Wean only after stability; plan pre-discharge safety assessment."]}]}, {"title": "Algorithm 2 — Persistent hypoglycemia discriminator", "h1": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Low BOHB + low FFA ± glucagon rise ≥30 mg/dL → HI (diazoxide ± chlorothiazide; echo; failure → genetics, octreotide, PET).", "Low BOHB + high FFA + high CK/ALT → FAOD (acylcarnitine; avoid fasting).", "High lactate + hepatomegaly + high uric acid → GSD I.", "Jaundice + sepsis + cataract + reducing substance → galactosemia (stop lactose).", "Midline defect / micropenis / cholestasis → pituitary axis, cortisol, hydrocortisone.", "High-AG ketoacidosis → organic acidemia."]}]}, {"title": "Algorithm 3 — Sodium emergency", "h1": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Confirm sodium and osmolality; assess shock, seizure, volume.", "Hyponatremic seizure → 3% saline 2–3 mL/kg over 10–20 min; target +4–6 mmol/L.", "Hypernatremic shock → isotonic bolus; then deficit over ≥48 h at ≤0.5 mmol/L/h.", "Hyponatremia + hyperkalemia + hypoglycemia → CAH: saline, dextrose, stress hydrocortisone.", "Paired urine studies separate SIADH, salt wasting and DI; repair the prescription or feeding cause."]}]}, {"title": "Algorithm 4 — Hyperkalemia", "h1": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Repeat K⁺ on free-flowing sample; ECG.", "ECG change or K⁺ &gt;6.5: calcium gluconate 1 mL/kg slowly.", "Shift: salbutamol or insulin–dextrose with glucose monitoring.", "Remove: furosemide if urine output; dialysis if refractory; treat cause (CAH, renal)."]}]}, {"title": "Algorithm 5 — Encephalopathy / suspected IEM", "h1": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["ABCs, glucose, gas, lactate, ketones, electrolytes, urgent ammonia.", "Respiratory alkalosis + ammonia ↑ → UCD; high-AG ketoacidosis → organic acidemia.", "Stop protein, start calories, give scavengers and arrange dialysis by ammonia level and response.", "Draw metabolic samples without delaying treatment."]}]}]}, {"id": "cases", "title": "Clinical Cases", "group": "Practice", "kind": "cases", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Case</strong>", "<strong>Stem</strong>", "<strong>Decision and why</strong>", "<strong>Trap</strong>"], "rows": [["<strong>1 Transitional</strong>", "38-wk IDM, 3 h, well, glucose 2.2 mmol/L.", "Feed + gel 0.5 mL/kg, recheck 30 min; IV if symptomatic.", "CPS threshold ≠ PES target."], ["<strong>2 Hidden HI</strong>", "Day 4, GIR 12, glucose 39, BOHB 0.1, FFA 0.2, insulin undetectable.", "Insulin action shown by fuels; critical sample and specialist review.", "Undetectable insulin does not exclude HI."], ["<strong>3 FAOD</strong>", "Day 5, fasting hypoglycemia, low ketones, FFA high, CK high, sibling death.", "Maintain glucose, avoid fasting, acylcarnitine.", "Low ketones occur in HI and FAOD; FFA separates."], ["<strong>4 Refractory low Ca</strong>", "Day 6, jitteriness, low iCa, Mg 0.4 mmol/L, high P.", "Monitored calcium and Mg; review feed, PTH.", "Low Mg blocks PTH."], ["<strong>5 Hypernatremia</strong>", "Day 9, 14% weight loss, Na 167, concentrated urine.", "Treat shock, replace deficit over ≥48 h, support lactation.", "Concentrated urine argues against DI."], ["<strong>6 CNS sodium</strong>", "Day 4 meningitis, Na 118, seizures.", "3% saline rescue, then cause-directed correction.", "Rescue ≠ normalisation."], ["<strong>7 UCD</strong>", "Day 4, post-feed encephalopathy, ammonia 700, respiratory alkalosis.", "Stop protein, calories, scavengers, dialysis pathway.", "Not every hyperammonemia is UCD."], ["<strong>8 CAH</strong>", "Day 6, Na 121, K⁺ 6.9, glucose 38, hyperpigmentation.", "Saline, dextrose, stress hydrocortisone after samples.", "Hyperkalemia with hyponatremia ≠ SIADH."]]}]}]}, {"id": "data", "title": "Data Interpretation", "group": "Practice", "kind": "data", "sections": [{"title": "Visual Station 1 — Hypoglycemia framework timeline", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic/tracing; not a patient image or recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: A well at-risk infant is 6 hours old with glucose 43 mg/dL. Which value is an intervention threshold and which is a treatment target?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image2.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Interpretation: CPS &lt;2.6 mmol/L in the first 72 h is a transitional intervention anchor; PES &gt;50 mg/dL before 48 h is a treatment target."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Decisive visual/data features: The graphic separates the age windows and labels threshold versus target."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Closest distractor / discriminator: Do not convert a PES target into an automatic IV-bolus trigger for every well transitional infant."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next management step: Use symptoms, feeding ability and the named framework; document a post-intervention glucose."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Exam trap: A number without age, symptoms and framework is incomplete."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Trace-back: Part I §2; Q8, Q22, Q49."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 2 — Critical-sample fuel pattern", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic/tracing; not a patient image or recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: Which pattern best separates HI from FAOD when both are hypoketotic?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image3.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Interpretation: Free fatty acids: suppressed in HI; usually elevated in FAOD."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Decisive visual/data features: HI suppresses BOHB and FFA; FAOD blocks ketogenesis despite ongoing lipolysis."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Closest distractor / discriminator: Low ketones alone do not distinguish the two."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next management step: Maintain glucose; complete the critical sample and targeted endocrine/metabolic work-up."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Exam trap: Undetectable insulin does not exclude HI when insulin action is evident."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Trace-back: Part I §4; Q28–30, Q50, Q71, Q77."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 3 — Electrolyte ECG pattern recognition", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic/tracing; not a patient image or recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: Match peaked T/widening QRS, prolonged QTc, and flat T with U wave to the electrolyte disorder."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image4_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image4.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Interpretation: Hyperkalemia → peaked T then conduction slowing; hypocalcemia → prolonged ST/QTc; hypokalemia → flat T/U wave."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Decisive visual/data features: Repolarization and conduction abnormalities differ by electrolyte."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Closest distractor / discriminator: Long QTc points to hypocalcemia, whereas a U wave points to hypokalemia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next management step: Confirm the electrolyte and treat ECG-toxic hyperkalemia/symptomatic hypocalcemia promptly."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Exam trap: Calcium stabilizes the myocardium in hyperkalemia but does not lower K⁺."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Trace-back: Part I §5/§7; Q20, Q31–33, Q55."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 4 — Chronic hypernatremia trajectory", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic/tracing; not a patient image or recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: Which line is safer and why?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image5_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image5.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Interpretation: The slower trajectory is safer for chronic hypernatremia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Decisive visual/data features: The 24-hour projected sodium fall, not the isolated sodium, determines osmotic safety."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Closest distractor / discriminator: Shock still receives isotonic resuscitation before slow correction of the remaining deficit."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next management step: Recalculate the sodium slope after each result and adjust the prescription."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Exam trap: Hypernatremia is not a reason to withhold indicated shock resuscitation."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Trace-back: Part I §6; Q38–40, Q48, Q61–63, Q73."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 5 — Blood gas + ammonia discriminator", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic/tracing; not a patient image or recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: Which infant fits UCD and which fits organic acidemia?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/gen_bloodgas_table.png", "alt": "", "caption": "Generated radiograph-style teaching schematic — not a patient image."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image6.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Interpretation: Infant X fits UCD; Infant Y fits organic acidemia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Decisive visual/data features: UCD commonly gives respiratory alkalosis with very high ammonia and little ketosis; organic acidemia gives high-AG ketoacidosis."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Closest distractor / discriminator: Hyperammonemia alone does not prove a UCD."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next management step: Start anti-catabolic emergency care while metabolic samples are processed."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Exam trap: Do not wait for genetics before treating severe hyperammonemia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Trace-back: Part I §8; Q17, Q43–46, Q64–66, Q74."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 6 — aEEG plus raw EEG retest", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational schematic/tracing; not a patient image or recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: Does the highlighted event represent seizure or artifact?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image7_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image7.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Interpretation: Evolving rhythmic raw-EEG activity with a concordant aEEG rise supports an electrographic seizure."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Decisive visual/data features: Evolution on the raw trace with stable impedance is the decisive feature."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Closest distractor / discriminator: Abrupt non-evolving activity with impedance change favors artifact; aEEG alone is insufficient."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next management step: Confirm seizure electrographically and assess reversible metabolic causes."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Exam trap: Do not diagnose from the compressed aEEG trend alone."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Trace-back: Notebook R1; Q19."}]}]}, {"id": "revision", "title": "Rapid Revision", "group": "Practice", "kind": "revision", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "• CPS 2025: first 72 h = transitional phase; &lt;2.6 mmol/L is an intervention anchor for at-risk infants. After 72 h, target ≥3.3 mmol/L and investigate persistent disease."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• PES: &gt;50 mg/dL before 48 h and &gt;60 mg/dL after 48 h are treatment targets; do not convert them into universal IV-bolus triggers."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• 40% dextrose gel 0.5 mL/kg = 200 mg/kg. Symptomatic/severe hypoglycemia → prompt IV glucose; do not delay for laboratory confirmation."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• GIR = D%×mL/kg/day÷144 or D%×10×mL/h÷(kg×60). GIR &gt;8 mg/kg/min is a clue to HI."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• HI: suppressed BOHB + FFA and a large glucagon response; insulin may be below assay detection. FAOD: low ketones with relatively high FFA."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• Diazoxide for established HI: 5–15 mg/kg/day; assess fluid retention and pulmonary-hypertension risk. Specialist second-line treatment includes somatostatin analogues."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• Early low Ca: preterm/IDM/asphyxia. Late low Ca: phosphate load, vitamin D/PTH/Mg disorders. Refractory low Ca → check Mg."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• Hyperkalemia: confirmed non-haemolysed K &gt;6.5 mmol/L or ECG toxicity. Calcium stabilizes membrane; insulin–dextrose/salbutamol shift K; removal follows."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• Hypernatremia: shock first if present; remaining correction generally ≤0.5 mmol/L/h and ≤10–12 mmol/L/day. Concentrated urine argues against DI."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• Hyponatremic encephalopathy: use the named protocol. NICE uses 2.7% saline 2 mL/kg; many Indian/neonatal protocols use 3% ~2–3 mL/kg."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• SIADH versus cerebral salt wasting: both may have concentrated urine/high urine Na; volume status is the key discriminator."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• UCD: respiratory alkalosis + marked ammonia. Organic acidemia: high-AG ketoacidosis ± cytopenia and secondary hyperammonemia."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• Severe hyperammonemia: brief protein cessation, anti-catabolic calories, scavenger pathway and early extracorporeal-clearance discussion when ammonia is very high/rising or encephalopathy is severe."}, {"t": "p", "html": "• Notebook R1 today: Q19 seizure pattern and Q54 preterm nutrition target. Score both separately from the 75."}]}]}, {"id": "pearls", "title": "Pearls & Traps", "group": "Practice", "kind": "pearls", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["A symptomatic neonate gets glucose immediately; laboratory confirmation must not delay rescue.", "CPS uses 72 h; PES uses 48 h. CPS 2.6 mmol/L is an intervention anchor; PES values are treatment targets.", "Gel 0.5 mL/kg = 200 mg/kg = D10 2 mL/kg in glucose mass; route and indication differ.", "GIR &gt; 8 mg/kg/min suggests HI; undetectable insulin does not exclude it; glucagon rise ≥30 mg/dL supports it.", "Low ketones + high FFA = FAOD; low ketones + low FFA = HI; high lactate + hepatomegaly = GSD I.", "Diazoxide: echo first; add chlorothiazide; unresponsive → genetics, octreotide, ¹⁸F-DOPA PET.", "Ionised calcium is the active fraction; low Mg makes calcium therapy fail; calcium chloride only centrally.", "Hypocalcemia + conotruncal defect = 22q11.2 deletion.", "Sodium: rate matters; hypernatremia ≤0.5 mmol/L/h; seizures in hyponatremia need 3% saline 2–3 mL/kg.", "Hyperkalemia: calcium stabilises, insulin–dextrose/salbutamol shifts, furosemide/dialysis removes.", "Hyponatremia + hyperkalemia + hypoglycemia = CAH until proven otherwise.", "Winter’s formula before calling compensation “appropriate”.", "Respiratory alkalosis + encephalopathy → ammonia; UCD: orotic acid high in OTC, low in CPS1.", "Ammonia &gt;400 or rising on scavengers → haemodialysis; peritoneal dialysis is the weakest option.", "Protein restriction in IEM is temporary (24–48 h)."]}]}, {"title": "Ten close-distractor traps", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["“All glucose &lt;50 mg/dL needs an IV bolus.” Wrong without age, symptoms and framework.", "“A normal insulin level excludes HI.” False when fuels and glucagon show insulin action.", "“Hypoketotic hypoglycemia is always HI.” FAOD mimics it.", "“More calcium fixes refractory hypocalcemia.” Check magnesium.", "“Hypernatremia means no IV fluids.” Shock still needs isotonic fluid.", "“Concentrated urine proves DI.” It argues against DI.", "“High urine sodium proves SIADH.” Salt wasting is the same.", "“Hyperglycemia raises measured sodium.” It lowers it; correct upward.", "“Any hyperammonemia is UCD.” Organic acidemia, GLUD1, THAN and liver disease also raise ammonia.", "“Insulin–dextrose only needs a single dextrose bolus.” Insulin outlasts it; run an infusion and check glucose."]}]}]}, {"id": "quizzes", "title": "Quizzes", "group": "Practice", "kind": "quizzes", "sections": []}, {"id": "recall", "title": "Active Recall", "group": "Revise", "kind": "recall", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "items": ["CPS vs PES age cut-offs?<br>Answer: CPS 72 h; PES 48 h.", "CPS transitional intervention threshold?<br>Answer: &lt;2.6 mmol/L (47 mg/dL); route by symptoms.", "CPS persistent-phase target?<br>Answer: ≥3.3 mmol/L (60 mg/dL) after 72 h.", "40% gel dose and glucose mass?<br>Answer: 0.5 mL/kg = 200 mg/kg, buccal with a feed.", "Two GIR formulas?<br>Answer: D% × mL/kg/day ÷ 144; D% × 10 × mL/h ÷ (kg × 60).", "D10 at 100 mL/kg/day gives what GIR?<br>Answer: 6.9 mg/kg/min.", "HI critical-sample criteria?<br>Answer: BOHB &lt;1.8, FFA &lt;1.7 mmol/L, glucagon rise ≥30 mg/dL at glucose &lt;50.", "FAOD vs HI?<br>Answer: FAOD: FFA high with low ketones; HI: both low.", "Diazoxide dose and key precaution?<br>Answer: 5–15 mg/kg/day; echo for pulmonary hypertension; with chlorothiazide.", "Features of hypopituitarism?<br>Answer: Midline defect, micropenis, cholestasis, recurrent hypoglycemia.", "Why does calcium fail to correct?<br>Answer: Hypomagnesemia (PTH failure).", "Hypocalcemia plus conotruncal heart disease?<br>Answer: 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge).", "Hypernatremia correction ceiling?<br>Answer: ≤0.5 mmol/L/h; ≤10–12 mmol/L/day.", "Hypertonic saline for seizure?<br>Answer: 3% saline 2–3 mL/kg over 10–20 min; raise 4–6 mmol/L.", "SIADH vs salt wasting?<br>Answer: Volume status: euvolaemic vs depleted.", "Hyperkalemia first step?<br>Answer: Calcium gluconate 1 mL/kg slowly with ECG monitor.", "AG for Na 140, Cl 108, HCO₃ 14?<br>Answer: 18 mmol/L.", "Winter’s for HCO₃ 14?<br>Answer: 1.5 × 14 + 8 = 29 ± 2 mmHg.", "OTC vs CPS1 in orotic acid?<br>Answer: OTC high orotic acid; CPS1 low.", "Dialysis trigger in hyperammonemia?<br>Answer: Ammonia &gt;400 µmol/L (or &gt;300 with coma), or rising despite scavengers; haemodialysis.", "From the ECG visual, what sequence suggests worsening hyperkalemia?<br>Answer: Peaked T → P-wave flattening/loss → QRS widening → sine wave/ventricular arrhythmia.", "On the sodium visual, what correction slope exceeds the usual ceiling?<br>Answer: Faster than about 0.5 mmol/L/h or more than 10–12 mmol/L/day.", "On the critical-sample visual, what single discriminator most helps HI vs FAOD?<br>Answer: FFA: low in HI, usually high in FAOD.", "On aEEG/raw EEG, what feature favors seizure over artifact?<br>Answer: Evolving rhythmic raw-EEG activity with stable impedance and a concordant aEEG trend."]}]}]}, {"id": "notebook", "title": "Error Notebook", "group": "Revise", "kind": "notebook", "sections": []}, {"id": "plan", "title": "Revision Plan", "group": "Revise", "kind": "plan", "sections": [{"title": "Self-Assessment — Red / Amber / Green", "h1": "Self-Assessment — Red / Amber / Green", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Domain</strong>", "<strong>Score</strong>", "<strong>RAG</strong>", "<strong>Next action</strong>"], "rows": [["Base MCQs", "____ / 75", "GREEN ≥85%; AMBER 70–84%; RED &lt;70%", "Repair every wrong/guessed item by discriminator."], ["Notebook Retests — Extra", "____ / 2", "GREEN only with secure fresh-stem reasoning", "Correct → R2; wrong → x2 failure and early repair."], ["Calculation Drill", "____ / 8", "GREEN ≥7; AMBER 5–6; RED ≤4", "Redo any miss with formula, units and arithmetic."], ["Active Recall", "____ / 24", "GREEN ≥21; AMBER 17–20; RED ≤16", "Repeat missed thresholds/patterns before the next day."], ["Visual/Data Stations", "____ / 6", "GREEN = can explain closest mimic unaided", "Re-open the visual then verbalize diagnosis + discriminator + next step."]]}]}]}, {"id": "references", "title": "References", "group": "More", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "Reference & Guideline Notes", "h1": "Reference & Guideline Notes", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd ed. — primary conceptual framework."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Canadian Paediatric Society. The screening and management of newborns at risk for low blood glucose. Updated 28 March 2025."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Thornton PS et al. Pediatric Endocrine Society recommendations for evaluation and management of persistent hypoglycemia. J Pediatr. 2015."}, {"t": "p", "html": "International Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Hyperinsulinism. Horm Res Paediatr.; available in PMC 2024. Diazoxide 5–15 mg/kg/day is first-line for established HI."}, {"t": "p", "html": "NICE NG29. Intravenous fluid therapy in children and young people in hospital. Term-neonate guidance includes minimal/no routine sodium before postnatal diuresis in critical adaptation and a hypernatremia correction limit of 12 mmol/L/day."}, {"t": "p", "html": "NHS Greater Glasgow &amp; Clyde neonatal hyperkalaemia guideline: common treatment threshold K⁺ &gt;6.5 mmol/L; ECG toxicity requires urgent treatment."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Consensus guidance for paediatric hyperammonemia and kidney replacement therapy: severe/rising hyperammonemia with neurologic deterioration requires early extracorporeal-clearance planning; &gt;400 µmol/L refractory to medical therapy is a major trigger."}, {"t": "p", "html": "India overlay: use current NNF/IAP/AIIMS/local NICU protocols for exact electrolyte replacement, hypertonic-saline concentration, glucose solutions and drug preparation. International and Indian frameworks are not silently mixed."}, {"t": "p", "html": "All medication doses are learner-facing exam anchors; verify the current neonatal formulary and unit protocol before bedside prescribing."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Final QA statement: locked base difficulty distribution retained — 7 Basic/Recall + 13 Conceptual + 27 Clinical/Application + 20 High-Difficulty + 8 Integrated/Rank-Differentiating = 75. Q19 and Q54 are extra; Calculation Drill is extra."}]}]}], "quizSets": [{"k": 1, "from": 1, "to": 16, "n": 16}, {"k": 2, "from": 17, "to": 32, "n": 16}, {"k": 3, "from": 33, "to": 47, "n": 15}, {"k": 4, "from": 48, "to": 62, "n": 15}, {"k": 5, "from": 63, "to": 77, "n": 15}], "mcqCount": 77, "calc": {"n": 8, "href": "calc.html"}};
